appas ahead by mid-single digits, and markets and most raters lean Democratic, though Cook and Inside Elections still call it a tossup. Sununu's name recognition and outside spending keep this competitive, so the forecast stays near the polling average rather than the more bullish fundamentals.", "watch": "A late shift in the national environment toward Republicans, or further outside spending boosting Sununu, could tighten this into a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Jeanne Shaheen (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Chris Pappas and John Sununu are competing for the open New Hampshire Senate seat left by Jeanne Shaheen's retirement. Pappas has out-raised Sununu by a wide margin, but Sununu benefits from roughly $2.9 million in outside support and carries strong statewide name recognition from his family's political history.\n\nPolls since mid-September consistently show Pappas leading by five to eight points, including surveys from the New York Times/Siena and the University of New Hampshire. Expert ratings split between Lean D, Likely D, and Tossup, and prediction markets price Democratic chances around 85 percent. The race remains genuinely contested given the national stakes for Senate control.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8721, "margin": 5.76}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8221, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7959, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8661, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8028, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.837, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8746, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8609, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.856, "margin": 6.0}]}